###  http://www.semanticweb.org/monic/ontologies/2026/0/untitled-ontology-120/stereotype_resilience
:stereotype_resilience rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Theory> ;
                       rdfs:comment "People tend to notice and remember stereotype-consistent information while ignoring contradictory evidence. This reinforces racial biases in crime perception, leading to overestimations of Black criminality. Such biases likely extend beyond individuals, shaping perceptions of predominantly Black neighborhoods as inherently dangerous, regardless of actual crime rates."@en ;
                       rdfs:label "stereotype resilience" ;
                       nudging:Demonstrates :african-americans_segregation_in_neighbourhoods .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/monic/ontologies/2026/0/untitled-ontology-120/stereotypes_and_group_perceptions
:stereotypes_and_group_perceptions rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Theory> ;
                                   rdfs:comment """- Some members of stereotyped groups internalize negative stereotypes. However, people tend to evaluate their own group more favorably .
- Black residents may use more nuanced judgments about young Black men compared to White residents"""@en ;
                                   rdfs:label "stereotypes and group perceptions" ;
                                   nudging:Demonstrates :african-americans_segregation_in_neighbourhoods .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/monic/ontologies/2026/0/untitled-ontology-120/stereotypical_bias
:stereotypical_bias rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Theory> ;
                    rdfs:comment "Survey respondents consistently rate blacks as more violence prone than any other American racial or ethnic group. The stereotype of blacks as criminals is widely known and is deeply embedded in the collective con- sciousness of Americans, irrespective of the level of prejudice or personal beliefs. In these theories, stereotypes arise from emotional needs to enhance or justify the position of one’s own racial group relative to other racial groups."@en ;
                    rdfs:label "stereotypical bias" ;
                    nudging:Demonstrates :african-americans_segregation_in_neighbourhoods .


